.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt PKG6REPO-RETIRE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pkg6repo retire
.Nd hide superseded package versions from repository catalogs
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm pkg6repo
.Cm retire plan
.Fl s Ar repo
.Op Fl -policy Ar file
.Op Fl -keep-file Ar file Ns ...
.Op Fl -fmri Ar selection Ns ...
.Op Fl -with-policy
.Op Fl o Ar planfile
.Op Fl F Cm table Ns | Ns Cm json
.Nm pkg6repo
.Cm retire apply
.Fl s Ar repo
.Op Fl -policy Ar file
.Op Fl -keep-file Ar file Ns ...
.Ar planfile
.Nm pkg6repo
.Cm retire restore
.Fl s Ar repo
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl -policy Ar file
.Ar selection Ns ...
.Nm pkg6repo
.Cm retire status
.Fl s Ar repo
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl -policy Ar file
.Op Fl -keep-file Ar file Ns ...
.Op Fl F Cm table Ns | Ns Cm json
.Nm pkg6repo
.Cm retire close-depot
.Fl s Ar repo
.Ar name
.Nm pkg6repo
.Cm retire check-policy
.Op Fl s Ar repo
.Op Fl -policy Ar file
.Op Fl -keep-file Ar file Ns ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Retirement is the first of two stages of repository retention.
A retired package version is left out of the catalogs and search indexes
that
.Xr pkg 1
clients download, so the client no longer considers it.
Its manifest and payloads stay in the repository:
.Nm pkg6depotd
keeps serving exact manifest and file
.Pq payload
requests for it, so installed systems can still verify and repair it.
Nothing is deleted.
There is no purge in this release.
.Pp
Retirement is unrelated to package obsoletion.
A package published with
.Sy pkg.obsolete=true
stays in the catalog, as in pkg5, and is never retired while it is the
newest version of its package.
.Pp
Only superseded versions are retired: a newer version of the same package
from the same publisher must exist.
Versions are ordered as pkg5 orders them: by release, then branch (the part
after
.Ql - ) ,
then timestamp.
.Pp
Versions are selected in one of two ways.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Policy candidates
If the policy enables retirement, a superseded version becomes a candidate
once the inactivity interval (12 months by default) has passed since the
latest of: its supersession (the first publication of a newer version), its
last qualifying access, its last restore, and the time since which access
history is proven complete (see
.Sx Access accounting ) .
.It Operator selections
Versions named with
.Fl -fmri .
The inactivity interval does not apply to them, and they may be retired
while policy-driven retirement is disabled.
.El
.Pp
A selection is either an exact FMRI, with publisher and complete
timestamped version, for example
.Ql pkg://openindiana.org/library/foo@1.0,5.11-2020.0.1.0:20200101T000000Z ,
or
.Ql regex: Ns Ar pattern ,
a regular expression matched against that form.
A selection that matches no package version is an error.
.Pp
A version matched by a
.Sy keep
rule of the policy or a keep file is never retired.
.Ss Checks
Retirement never makes a protected snapshot or a visible package
uninstallable.
A plan, an apply and a restore are refused, and nothing is changed, if:
.Bl -bullet
.It
a version to hide belongs to the closure of a protected
.Sy snapshot
(see
.Xr pkg6-policy 5 ) ;
.It
a snapshot version is already hidden;
.It
a package that stays visible has a
.Sy require ,
.Sy require-any
or
.Sy incorporate
dependency that only versions being hidden satisfy (obsolete versions do
not satisfy
.Sy require ) ;
.It
a version to restore depends on versions that stay hidden;
.It
a package version or dependency cannot be interpreted (a version that is
not a pkg5 version, a
.Sy depend
action of unknown type or without
.Sy fmri ) ,
and a version to hide is of a package it may need.
Such content is never skipped; it is also listed by
.Cm status .
.El
.Pp
Requirements of a protected snapshot that no version in the repository
satisfies are reported by
.Cm plan
and
.Cm status
as problems of that snapshot.
.Pp
The error lists each problem and the exact
.Ql keep Qq Ar fmri
entries to add to the policy or a keep file, or the versions to restore.
Review them before adding them.
.Ss Access accounting
.Nm pkg6depotd
records every
.Sy GET
of an exact manifest or payload of a repository managed through generations
in
.Pa retention/access/
below the repository.
Catalog, search, info, web interface and
.Sy HEAD
requests do not count.
A payload request counts for a package version only if no other version of
the publisher references the payload.
Access to a retired version resets its clock but leaves it retired.
.Pp
Retirement for inactivity counts only access history that is proven
complete, and fails closed where it is not.
Each depot records under a name made of its host name and listen address,
which stays the same across restarts and must differ between depots
serving the same repository.
It starts recording once it is ready to serve (with TLS, after its
certificate is loaded or issued), writes a heartbeat every minute while it
runs, and ends its records with a clean shutdown.
A stop whose requests do not finish within ten seconds is not clean.
The following make accesses possibly missing, and inactivity then counts
from their end only:
.Bl -bullet
.It
a failed write of an access record (a full disk, for example), until the
depot writes again;
.It
a depot that stopped without a clean shutdown, until it is restarted;
.It
access records that were deleted or truncated, or are unreadable,
including the newest records of a depot that has stopped: each depot keeps
the position of its newest heartbeat, start or shutdown record in
.Pa retention/access/head.* ,
and records that do not reach it count as missing.
.El
.Pp
A depot that has not written for ten minutes may be unable to write
anything; while it has not, no version is a policy candidate.
Restart it once it can write, or, if it is gone for good, close it with
.Cm close-depot .
Until a depot has recorded its first access, no version is a policy
candidate.
Periods in which no depot runs count as periods without access: this
assumes
.Nm pkg6depotd
is the only server of the repository.
.Pp
The depot must be able to write to
.Pa retention/access/ ;
if it cannot, it logs a warning and keeps serving: requests never fail
because of access accounting.
.Nm pkg6depotd
runs as
.Sy pkg6srv
and creates the directory when it starts.
Run
.Nm pkg6repo retire
as the same user.
.Cm apply
creates
.Pa retention/access/fence.lock
and
.Cm close-depot
creates the day's access log if they are missing; run as root, both give
what they create to the owner of the repository, and fail without
changing anything if they cannot.
A request that writes an access record waits until the record is on disk,
but never for a running
.Cm apply .
There is no option to evaluate the policy at another time than now.
.Sh SUBCOMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm plan
Compute a retirement plan against the published repository state and print
it.
Nothing is changed.
Without
.Fl -fmri ,
the plan holds the policy candidates; with
.Fl -fmri ,
only the selected versions, plus the policy candidates if
.Fl -with-policy
is given.
Versions that were selected or eligible but are not retired are listed with
the reason.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o Ar planfile
Write the plan as JSON for
.Cm apply .
.It Fl F Cm table Ns | Ns Cm json
Output format.
.El
.It Cm apply Ar planfile
Take the repository writer lock and check the plan again against the
repository as it is now, with current access records.
If the repository changed since the plan was made, the plan is checked
against the new state.
The plan is refused if it was edited, was made for another repository or
with a different policy or keep files, or if any of its versions was
removed, is no longer superseded, is now kept, or (for policy candidates)
was accessed since or is no longer covered by complete access history.
Otherwise one new repository generation with the updated catalogs and an
audit record is built.
For policy candidates,
.Cm apply
then waits (up to 30 seconds) until every running depot confirms that it
has recorded every access, reads the access records once more and checks
the candidates again; accesses recorded while it switches are held back by
the depots and recorded right after.
Only then is the generation published atomically.
Clients see either the old or the new catalogs.
Applying a plan again changes nothing.
.It Cm restore Ar selection Ns ...
Make hidden versions visible in catalogs and search again, in one new
generation with an audit record.
Selections that match only visible versions change nothing.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl n
Show what would be restored.
.El
.It Cm status
Show whether policy-driven retirement is enabled, the intervals, since when
access is tracked and proven complete, the number of gaps in the access
history, the depots recording access, the number of hidden versions, and
problems: snapshot problems and content whose dependencies cannot be
interpreted.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl v
Also list each hidden version with its last access, each gap in the access
history, and the audit trail.
.El
.It Cm close-depot Ar name
Declare that the depot
.Ar name ,
as
.Cm status
lists it, stopped without a clean shutdown and will not come back.
Access history is proven complete again from now on.
Refused while the depot is still writing records.
Do not close a depot that still runs but cannot write its records, for
example on a full disk: make it able to write and restart it instead.
If a closed depot writes records again, it was not gone: access history is
proven again only from its next record, and from before its failure if it
had failed writes.
.It Cm check-policy
Validate the policy and keep files, and print a summary.
The repository is not opened.
.El
.Pp
Options common to the subcommands:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl s Ar repo
The repository.
It must publish through repository generations, as repositories created or
adopted by
.Nm pkg6repo Cm import-pkg5
do.
.It Fl -policy Ar file
Read the policy from
.Ar file
instead of
.Pa policy.kdl
in the repository.
.It Fl -keep-file Ar file
Read additional keep rules from
.Ar file ,
in text, KDL or JSON form (see
.Xr pkg6-policy 5 ) .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa repo/policy.kdl
Default policy.
.It Pa repo/generations/current/retirement/state.json
Hidden versions of the published generation.
.It Pa repo/generations/current/retirement/audit/
One record per retirement or restore: operation, time, operator, plan,
policy digest and versions.
.It Pa repo/retention/access/
Access records and heartbeats written by
.Nm pkg6depotd .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std pkg6repo
A blocked, stale or refused operation exits non-zero and changes nothing.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Example 1 Reviewing and applying policy-driven retirement
Run as the depot's service user, which owns the repository:
.Bd -literal -offset 2n
# su pkg6srv -c '/usr/bin/pkg6repo retire plan \e
      -s /var/pkg6depotd/repo -o /var/pkg6depotd/plan.json'
# su pkg6srv -c '/usr/bin/pkg6repo retire apply \e
      -s /var/pkg6depotd/repo /var/pkg6depotd/plan.json'
.Ed
.Ss Example 2 Retiring selected versions
.Bd -literal -offset 2n
# su pkg6srv -c "/usr/bin/pkg6repo retire plan -s /var/pkg6depotd/repo \e
      --fmri 'regex:^pkg://openindiana.org/library/foo@1\e.' \e
      -o /var/pkg6depotd/plan.json"
.Ed
.Ss Example 3 Restoring a version
.Bd -literal -offset 2n
# su pkg6srv -c "/usr/bin/pkg6repo retire restore -s /var/pkg6depotd/repo \e
      'pkg://openindiana.org/library/foo@1.0,5.11-2020.0.1.0:20200101T000000Z'"
.Ed
.Sh INTERFACE STABILITY
.Sy Uncommitted
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pkg 1 ,
.Xr pkg6repo 1 ,
.Xr pkg6-policy 5 ,
.Xr pkg6depotd 8
